ic carbon to give the alpha-aminonitrile after proton transfer.", "reasoning": "Aminoacetonitrile is the formaldehyde-derived aminonitrile in the cyanosulfidic network of Patel et al. (2015). The paper treats it as part of a broader unified chemistry rather than an isolated single-flask Strecker study, but the intermediate is experimentally grounded and is the correct surface precursor to glycine. Evaporative concentration is important because HCN and NH3 must be locally high (Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Aminoacetonitrile hydrolysis to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Hydrolysis during wet phases after aminonitrile accumulation; bulk water treated implicitly rather than as a separate node"}, "mechanism": "The nitrile hydrates stepwise through an amide-like intermediate and then to the carboxylic acid, yielding glycine and released ammonia.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) showed aminonitriles hydrolyze to amino acids in aqueous phases; aminoacetonitrile gives glycine with ammonia release. Water is implicit as the bulk solvent/reactant and is omitted as a separate node. This route is strong surface chemistry because it converts a cyanide-stabilized intermediate into the amino acid during rehydration (Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Iron-promoted reductive amination of glyoxylate to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["elemental iron (Fe0)", "ferrous iron (Fe2+)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "343-353K", "pressure": "elevated submarine/subseafloor", "catalyst": "Fe0/Fe2+", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic vent or subseafloor water; hydroxylamine is the nitrogen donor"}, "mechanism": "Glyoxylate forms an N-bearing intermediate with hydroxylamine, and Fe0/Fe2+ supplies reducing power to convert the alpha-keto acid manifold into glycine.", "reasoning": "Muchowska et al. (2019) experimentally demonstrated Fe0/Fe2+-promoted conversion of glyoxylate and hydroxylamine to glycine at about 70 C. This is a well-supported hydrothermal entrance to the glycine hub. The main uncertainty is upstream hydroxylamine supply, not the iron-mediated chemistry itself (Muchowska et al., 2019)."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Fe2+-catalyzed cleavage of isocitrate to glyoxylate and succinate", "inputs": ["mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_015"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["ferrous iron (Fe2+)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "343-353K", "pressure": "elevated submarine/subseafloor", "catalyst": "Fe2+", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic hydrothermal water; TCA-like precursor cleavage"}, "mechanism": "Fe2+ promotes a retro-aldol-like cleavage of isocitrate, generating a C2 glyoxylate fragment and a C4 succinate fragment.", "reasoning": "Muchowska et al. (2019) showed Fe2+-driven cleavage of isocitrate to glyoxylate and succinate, providing a hydrothermal source of the C2 alpha-keto acid. The cleavage chemistry is experimentally solid, although prebiotic abundance of isocitrate is less certain than the reaction once isocitrate is available (Muchowska et al., 2019)."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "UV conversion of 2,2-dihydroxyacetic acid to glyoxylate", "inputs": ["mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["ultraviolet radiation"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "UV radiation", "aqueous": true, "other": "Shallow UV-irradiated pool; sulfite-compatible photoredox context; anoxic"}, "mechanism": "UV-driven dehydration and photoredox chemistry converts the gem-diol hydrate to free glyoxylate.", "reasoning": "Liu et al. (2021) demonstrated UV photoredox regeneration of glyoxylate from 2,2-dihydroxyacetic acid. This is a strong surface entry because early Earth UV was intense and shallow pools can continuously generate reactive glyoxylate locally (Liu et al., 2021)."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Peracetic acid amination to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "350-400K", "pressure": "elevated submarine/subseafloor", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Submarine hydrothermal fluid; anoxic; peroxide-bearing local chemistry"}, "mechanism": "Ammonolysis and substitution at the oxidized acetyl group produce the C-N bonded amino acid framework; peroxide-derived coproducts are omitted from the graph.", "reasoning": "Aubrey et al. (2008) reported glycine formation from peracetic acid and ammonia under submarine hydrothermal conditions. The exact byproduct manifold is simplified here, but the carbon-nitrogen bond-forming route to glycine is experimentally grounded. Its prebiotic importance depends on local availability of peroxide chemistry, so it is included as a supported alternative rather than a dominant route (Aubrey et al., 2008)."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Hydrothermal hydroxymethylation of glycine to serine", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "350-400K", "pressure": "elevated submarine/subseafloor", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Mild hydrothermal microenvironments; rapid cooling or export favors product survival"}, "mechanism": "An aldol-like hydroxymethylation installs a CH2OH group at the glycine alpha-carbon using formaldehyde as the C1 electrophile.", "reasoning": "Aubrey et al. (2008) directly observed serine formation from glycine and formaldehyde under hydrothermal conditions. This is one of the best-supported direct serine-forming reactions, but the review highlights two caveats: formaldehyde steady-state concentration is debated, and serine can decompose at prolonged high temperature. The route is therefore most plausible in mild vent fringes or rapidly quenched fluids rather than the hottest cores (Aubrey et al., 2008)."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Formation of N-methylene glycine from glycine and formaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "323K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Geothermal or evaporitic pool; condensation with implicit water loss; recurrent concentration helps imine formation"}, "mechanism": "The glycine amino group condenses with formaldehyde to form the Schiff base N-methylene glycine.", "reasoning": "Krishnamurthy et al. (2016) identified N-methylene glycine as the spontaneous Schiff base formed from glycine and formaldehyde at 50 C. Water elimination is implicit and not shown as a node. This mild surface step creates the key branching intermediate for the metal-free serine route (Krishnamurthy et al., 2016)."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Conversion of N-methylene glycine and formaldehyde to serine", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "323K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "About 24 h under mild heating; hydration and hydrolysis occur in bulk water, which is treated implicitly"}, "mechanism": "Formaldehyde adds to the imine or enamine manifold of N-methylene glycine, and subsequent hydration and hydrolysis yield serine.", "reasoning": "Krishnamurthy et al. (2016) showed N-methylene glycine plus a second formaldehyde equivalent yields serine in water at 50 C. This is a compelling surface pathway because it avoids mineral catalysts. The open question is selectivity, since the same chemistry can also furnish glyoxylate; the route is kept because serine is a documented major product under the reported conditions (Krishnamurthy et al., 2016)."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Strecker-type formation of serine aminonitrile from glycolaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Cyanosulfidic surface pool; wet-dry concentration with intermittent UV-driven feedstock renewal"}, "mechanism": "Glycolaldehyde and ammonia form an iminium equivalent, and cyanide addition gives 2-amino-3-hydroxypropanenitrile.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) embedded 2-amino-3-hydroxypropanenitrile in their cyanosulfidic protometabolic network as the direct aminonitrile precursor to serine from glycolaldehyde, HCN, and NH3. As with aminoacetonitrile, the chemistry is strongest in concentrated surface pools where cyanide chemistry can cycle between dry and wet phases (Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Hydrolysis of serine aminonitrile to serine", "inputs": ["mol_014"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Wet phase after aminonitrile build-up; bulk water treated implicitly; reduced UV exposure favors survival to hydrolysis"}, "mechanism": "The nitrile hydrolyzes through an amide-like intermediate to the carboxylic acid, furnishing serine and released ammonia.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) demonstrated hydrolytic conversion of 2-amino-3-hydroxypropanenitrile to serine with ammonia release. Water is implicit in the aqueous setting and omitted as a node. A remaining limitation is aminonitrile stability under prolonged UV exposure, so formation during concentrated phases followed by hydrolysis in later wet phases is the most plausible scenario (Patel et al., 2015)."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Isocitrate cleavage to serine by direct aldol chemistry", "description": "Hydrothermal Fe2+-mediated isocitrate cleavage feeds glyoxylate, which is reductively aminated to glycine and then hydroxymethylated by formaldehyde to serine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_004", "rxn_003", "rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_007"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Isocitrate cleavage to serine via N-methylene glycine", "description": "The same hydrothermal glyoxylate source is converted to glycine and then reaches serine through the mild surface Schiff-base relay.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_004", "rxn_003", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_008"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "UV glyoxylate route to serine by direct aldol chemistry", "description": "Surface UV chemistry produces glyoxylate from its hydrate, after which iron-mediated glycine synthesis and direct glycine hydroxymethylation furnish serine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_005", "rxn_003", "rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_007"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "UV glyoxylate route to serine via N-methylene glycine", "description": "Surface-generated glyoxylate is converted to glycine and then to serine through the N-methylene glycine intermediate under mild surface conditions.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_005", "rxn_003", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_008"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Formaldehyde Strecker glycine route to serine by direct aldol chemistry", "description": "Formaldehyde enters a cyanosulfidic Strecker sequence to aminoacetonitrile; hydrolysis gives glycine, followed by direct hydroxymethylation to serine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_007"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Formaldehyde Strecker glycine route to serine via N-methylene glycine", "description": "The same aminoacetonitrile-to-glycine branch reaches serine through Schiff-base formation with formaldehyde and subsequent mild surface conversion.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001",