"common_name": "N-methylene glycine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H5NO2/c1-4-2-3(5)6/h1-2H2,(H,5,6)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C3H6N2O", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-hydroxypropanenitrile", "common_name": "serine aminonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6N2O/c4-1-3(5)2-6/h3,6H,2,5H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C3H7NO3", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-hydroxypropanoic acid", "common_name": "serine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO3/c4-2(1-5)3(6)7/h2,5H,1,4H2,(H,6,7)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C2H4O4", "iupac_name": "2,2-dihydroxyacetic acid", "common_name": "glyoxylic acid hydrate", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O4/c3-1(4)2(5)6/h3-4H,(H,5,6)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C2H4O3", "iupac_name": "peroxyacetic acid", "common_name": "peracetic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O3/c1-2(3)5-4/h4H,1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Strecker formation of aminoacetonitrile from formaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_003", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "cyanide-rich evaporitic or geothermal pool; wet-dry concentration compatible with cyanosulfidic chemistry"}, "mechanism": "Formaldehyde first forms an imine or iminium with ammonia; cyanide adds to the electrophilic carbon to give the aminonitrile, with net loss of water.", "reasoning": "This is the standard Strecker entry to aminoacetonitrile within cyanosulfidic surface chemistry and is supported as the glycine-precursor branch in Patel et al. (2015). Surface pools are favored because they allow HCN accumulation and wet-dry concentration. H2O is included explicitly as the condensation coproduct."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Hydrolysis of aminoacetonitrile to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "rehydrating phase after cyanide concentration; neutral to mildly basic aqueous conditions"}, "mechanism": "The nitrile is hydrated stepwise through amide-like intermediates and then hydrolyzed to the carboxylic acid, releasing ammonia.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) place aminoacetonitrile hydrolysis on a credible path from HCN chemistry to amino acids. The full hydrolysis consumes two waters, but coefficients are not encoded in this schema; H2O is therefore listed once to mark the required hydrolytic participation."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Iron-promoted reductive amination of glyoxylate to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["elemental iron (Fe0)", "ferrous iron (Fe2+)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-360K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "Fe0/Fe2+", "aqueous": true, "other": "H2-rich subseafloor vent or iron-rich hydrothermal reactor; H2 is included for redox bookkeeping alongside iron-promoted reduction"}, "mechanism": "Hydroxylamine first forms the oxime or imine equivalent of glyoxylate, which is then reduced under iron-promoted H2-rich conditions to the amino acid; water is released in the overall sequence.", "reasoning": "Muchowska et al. (2019) experimentally showed iron-promoted reductive amination and transamination chemistry converting alpha-keto acids to amino acids, including glyoxylate to glycine. Here H2 is included explicitly for redox bookkeeping under H2-rich hydrothermal conditions, while Fe0 and Fe2+ are retained as the experimentally supported promoters. The overall net reaction produces two waters in full stoichiometric form, but coefficients are not represented."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Hydrothermal amination of peracetic acid to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "340-380K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "short residence times in submarine hydrothermal fluid mixing zones"}, "mechanism": "Ammonia attacks the activated carbonyl of peracetic acid, and the adduct collapses through peroxide loss and hydrolysis to give glycine.", "reasoning": "Aubrey, Cleaves, and Bada (2008) reported glycine formation from peracetic acid and ammonia under submarine hydrothermal conditions. This branch is retained because it is experimentally reported, but its prebiotic weight is lower than HCN- or glyoxylate-based branches because sustained supplies of peracetic acid under anoxic conditions are less secure. H2O is included explicitly as the coproduct."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Schiff-base formation of N-methylene glycine from glycine and formaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "320-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "mildly warm geothermal or evaporitic pool; evaporation favors imine formation"}, "mechanism": "The amino group of glycine condenses with formaldehyde to form the Schiff base or iminium N-methylene glycine, releasing water.", "reasoning": "Krishnamurthy et al. (2016) identified N-methylene glycine as a plausible intermediate formed spontaneously from glycine and formaldehyde at about 323 K. This step is most plausible in surface pools where evaporation can offset water activity and favor condensation; formaldehyde concentration is the main bottleneck. When glycine is generated hydrothermally upstream, the mixed pathways in this network explicitly assume transport and concentration in surface pools before this step."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Hydrolytic rearrangement of N-methylene glycine to serine", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "320-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "hydrating phase of geothermal cycling; reaction competes with glyoxylate-forming channels"}, "mechanism": "Hydration and proton-shift rearrangement of the preformed C3 imine converts it into the beta-hydroxy amino acid framework of serine.", "reasoning": "Krishnamurthy et al. (2016) support N-methylene glycine as the proximate intermediate in nonenzymatic serine formation from glycine and formaldehyde, but the often-summarized net step N-methylene glycine + formaldehyde + H2O -> serine is not carbon-conserving. The node is therefore written as a mass-balanced hydrolytic conversion of the already C3 imine intermediate to serine. This standalone formulation is partially speculative, but it respects atom balance and preserves the mechanistic role of N-methylene glycine; the competition between serine formation and glyoxylate production remains an open selectivity problem (Krishnamurthy et al., 2016)."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Hydrothermal glycine-formaldehyde hydroxymethylation to serine", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "350-380K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "mild submarine hydrothermal mixing conditions; short residence times limit retro-aldol decomposition"}, "mechanism": "The alpha-carbon of glycine, accessed as a glycinate or enolizable equivalent, adds to formaldehyde in an aldol-type hydroxymethylation to produce serine.", "reasoning": "Aubrey, Cleaves, and Bada (2008) experimentally demonstrated serine formation from glycine and formaldehyde under submarine hydrothermal conditions. This is one of the strongest direct routes in the serine literature, but it depends on localized formaldehyde supply and on residence times short enough to avoid thermal retro-aldol loss of serine back to glycine and formaldehyde, a caveat highlighted in the review. Where glycine is supplied by surface HCN chemistry upstream, the corresponding mixed pathways explicitly require delivery into hydrothermal reaction zones."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Strecker formation of serine aminonitrile from glycolaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_003", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "cyanosulfidic surface pool with wet-dry concentration and intermittent UV-driven precursor supply"}, "mechanism": "Glycolaldehyde forms an imine or iminium with ammonia; cyanide addition then furnishes the aminonitrile corresponding to serine.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) place 2-amino-3-hydroxypropanenitrile within a unified cyanosulfidic network starting from HCN-derived chemistry and simple carbonyl compounds such as glycolaldehyde. Surface pools are favored because they enable concentration, photochemical precursor generation, and later hydrolysis. H2O is written explicitly as the condensation coproduct."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "UV dehydration of glyoxylic acid hydrate to glyoxylate", "inputs": ["mol_014"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["UV radiation"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "UV-irradiated shallow pool; photoredox dehydration of glyoxylic acid hydrate"}, "mechanism": "UV light dehydrates the gem-diol form of glyoxylic acid to the aldehydic glyoxylate.", "reasoning": "Liu et al. (2021) experimentally demonstrated UV-driven conversion of 2,2-dihydroxyacetic acid to glyoxylate. This branch is especially relevant for ozone-free surface environments, where UV flux both threatens and replenishes organics; here it feeds the glyoxylate hub that can be aminated to glycine."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Hydrolysis of serine aminonitrile to serine", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "hydrating phase after aminonitrile accumulation; neutral to mildly basic aqueous conditions"}, "mechanism": "The nitrile is hydrated through amide-like intermediates and then hydrolyzed to the amino acid, releasing ammonia.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) support hydrolysis of 2-amino-3-hydroxypropanenitrile to serine in aqueous surface settings. As with other nitrile hydrolyses here, the fully balanced stoichiometry consumes two waters overall; H2O is listed once because coefficients are not represented. The review notes that the stability of this aminonitrile under prolonged UV exposure remains an open question."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Cyanosulfidic glycine followed by hydrothermal hydroxymethylation", "description": "Formaldehyde enters surface Strecker chemistry with HCN and NH3 to give aminoacetonitrile; rehydration yields glycine, which is then transferred to a mild hydrothermal mixing zone for direct hydroxymethylation by formaldehyde.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Cyanosulfidic glycine followed by the surface N-methylene route", "description": "The same Strecker-generated glycine remains in warm surface pools, condenses with formaldehyde to N-methylene glycine, and hydrolytic rearrangement yields serine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_005", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_010", "mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Hydrothermal glyoxylate amination to direct serine", "description": "Hydrothermal Fe and H2 chemistry reductively aminates glyoxylate to glycine, followed by direct hydrothermal hydroxymethylation of glycine by formaldehyde.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003", "rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Hydrothermal glyoxylate amination with surface imine finishing", "description": "Hydrothermal glyoxylate amination makes glycine, but the final serine-forming chemistry occurs after transfer to a surface pool where N-methylene glycine is generated and hydrolyzed.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003", "rxn_005", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_010", "mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "UV glyoxylate-hydrate branch to hydrothermal serine", "description": "UV irradiation of glyoxylic acid hydrate creates glyoxylate at the surface; glyoxylate is then delivered to hydrothermal Fe and H2 chemistry for conversion to glycine and onward to serine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_009", "rxn_003", "rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "UV glyoxylate-hydrate branch with surface imine finishing", "description": "Surface UV first makes glyoxylate, hydrothermal Fe and H2 chemistry converts it to glycine, and geothermal cycling returns the glycine to surface pools for the N-methylene glycine route.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_009", "rxn_003", "rxn_005", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_010", "mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Peracetic acid hydrothermal branch to serine", "description": "Peracetic acid and ammonia generate glycine under submarine hydrothermal conditions, and the glycine is then directly hydroxymethylated by formaldehyde to serine; the oxidant supply is likely episodic rather than continuous.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_004", "rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Peracetic acid branch with surface N-methylene finishing", "description": "Peracetic acid hydrothermally supplies glycine, which is then transported to surface pools and converted to serine through N-methylene glycine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_010", "mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Direct cyanosulfidic serine aminonitrile hydrolysis", "description": "Glycolaldehyde undergoes Strecker-type cyanosulfidic conversion to serine aminonitrile in a surface pool, followed by aqueous hydrolysis to serine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_008", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Direct hydrothermal glycine-formaldehyde aldol route", "description": "An existing glycine pool reacts directly with formaldehyde in a mild hydrothermal reactor to give serine; this is the shortest experimentally demonstrated glycine-based suffix in the network.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": []}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_005", "mol_008", "mol_010"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_005", "incoming_reactions": [], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_001", "rxn_005", "rxn_007"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_010", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_005", "rxn_007"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_013", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_010"], "outgoing_reactions": []}]}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 540875.8978843689, "success": true, "error_message": null}
