rey et al. (2008) observed serine formation from glycine and formaldehyde under submarine hydrothermal conditions. The main caveat is serine thermal lability and possible retro-aldol decomposition back to glycine plus formaldehyde at prolonged high temperature, noted in the review (Aubrey et al., 2008; Bada et al., 1978), so the most plausible window is mild hydrothermal heating with rapid quench."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Schiff-base formation of N-methylene glycine", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_004", "mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "323K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "warm formaldehyde-rich geothermal or evaporitic pool; concentration by wet-dry cycling"}, "mechanism": "Condensation of the glycine amino group with formaldehyde forms an imine (Schiff base) and releases water.", "reasoning": "Krishnamurthy et al. (2016) showed that glycine and formaldehyde spontaneously form N-methylene glycine in warm aqueous solution. This provides the experimentally anchored imine node for the surface serine branch, although maintaining sufficient local formaldehyde remains an open prebiotic concentration problem."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Net conversion of N-methylene glycine to serine", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "323K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "warm aqueous pool; encoded as the net serine-forming branch of dynamic iminium chemistry over about 24 h"}, "mechanism": "Net hydrolytic rearrangement and prototropic reorganization of the preformed C3 imine gives serine.", "reasoning": "Krishnamurthy et al. (2016) reported serine among the products of N-methylene-glycine/formaldehyde aqueous chemistry near 323 K. To keep the graph mass-balanced, this node is written as the net conversion of the already assembled C3 imine to serine; the extra formaldehyde discussed mechanistically is treated as part of the prior iminium equilibrium rather than as a separate net carbon input. The route is therefore literature-guided but intentionally compressed, and the experimentally observed competition with glyoxylate formation remains an open selectivity problem."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Strecker formation of serine aminonitrile from glycolaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_015", "mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "HCN/NH3-rich cyanosulfidic pools with evaporative concentration"}, "mechanism": "Strecker-type imine formation at glycolaldehyde followed by cyanide addition gives the beta-hydroxyl aminonitrile.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) place 2-amino-3-hydroxypropanenitrile in the cyanosulfidic network from glycolaldehyde, HCN, and NH3. This is the most direct experimentally supported entry into serine precursor space on the surface and bypasses the free-glycine hub."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Serine aminonitrile hydrolysis to serine", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_001", "mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "wet-phase hydrolysis in cyanosulfidic pools; overall balanced form uses 2 H2O equivalents"}, "mechanism": "Sequential nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis convert the aminonitrile into the amino acid with release of ammonia.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) showed that 2-amino-3-hydroxypropanenitrile hydrolyzes to serine and ammonia in water. The overall hydrolysis consumes two water equivalents, and the review notes that aminonitrile persistence under intense UV and fluctuating pH is still a genuine uncertainty."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Surface Strecker 